Reading A: a site holds only the derivation's outputs (carrier, stride).

swept box: widths 1..=128, four strategies, one sign  =  512 declarations
distinct (carrier, stride) classes                    =  128
classes carrying more than one declared width         =  5
declarations whose W IS recoverable from the pair     =  123 of 512
declarations whose W is NOT recoverable from the pair =  389 of 512  (76.0%)

the largest ambiguous classes:
  carrier 128 bits, stride 128 bits  <-  64 widths, 65..=128
  carrier  64 bits, stride  64 bits  <-  32 widths, 33..=64
  carrier  32 bits, stride  32 bits  <-  16 widths, 17..=32
  carrier   8 bits, stride   8 bits  <-  8 widths, 1..=8
  carrier  16 bits, stride  16 bits  <-  8 widths, 9..=16

restricted to Cold alone: 128 classes over 128 widths, ambiguous classes 0
  so W IS recoverable from the pair when the strategy packs at stride == W,
  and that recovery is what the unpacked strategies destroy.

what a site that has lost W cannot then do, at one worked declaration:
  UFixed<13,0,Warm> and UFixed<16,0,Warm> both present as (carrier 16, stride 16)
  a multiply must shift right by F and mask to W bits. both are functions of the
  declaration, and neither is a function of (carrier, stride). so under Reading A
  the criterion's clause one has excluded a fact the machine needs and the site
  cannot reach. that is not a count being wrong. it is the reading being unsound.
